-- ***** SOUNDNESS WITNESS -- read before touching postcondition propagation *****
--
-- Refinement types, Tier 0 postcondition-propagation gate (§2.14; see also
-- specs/progress.md "Tier 0 postcondition propagation" and the "unverified
-- relational postcondition does not propagate" case in test/test_refinecheck.ml).
--
-- `shady`'s declared postcondition `{Int | _ < n}` is checked against its own
-- BODY at the definition site: `shady` just forwards to `opaque_id`, an
-- unanalysable call (refinecheck does not inline or symbolically execute
-- arbitrary callees -- only measures and a handful of known constructs are
-- reflected into Z3). Because the body cannot be shown to satisfy `_ < n`,
-- the proof obligation FAILS to discharge at the definition, and
-- `gate_unverified_posts` (lib/refinecheck) strips the postcondition before
-- it is recorded in `postcond_of` for callers. A postcondition is a FACT
-- refinecheck itself proved, never a trusted contract taken on faith from the
-- signature -- otherwise a function could declare an arbitrarily strong lie
-- and every caller would inherit it unchecked.
--
-- The call `takepos(shady(0))` is therefore evaluated with NO knowledge of
-- what `shady` returns beyond its erased base type `Int`. `takepos` demands
-- `_ >= 0`; an unconstrained `Int` neither proves nor refutes that, so
-- refinecheck stays silent -- this file must `--check` exit 0.
--
-- WHAT BREAKS IF THIS EVER FLIPS: if a future change makes an unverified
-- postcondition propagate anyway (e.g. by treating any declared refinement
-- as a trusted contract, or by weakening `gate_unverified_posts` to pass
-- through partially-analysed bodies), `shady(0)`'s instantiated postcondition
-- would read `_ < 0`, which DOES contradict `takepos`'s `_ >= 0` -- and this
-- exact call, correct today, would start being reported. That is a false
-- positive on code that was never wrong; this witness exists to catch it the
-- moment it happens. Do not "fix" this file by editing `shady` to make its
-- postcondition provable -- that would defeat the point of the test.
--
-- (2026-08-30, counterexample surfacing: the body must also make the
-- postcondition TRUE, not just unprovable -- `shady` returning `opaque_id(n)`
-- unchanged was refutable by execution (shady(0) = 0, and 0 < 0 fails), so
-- the witness validator correctly rejected the DEFINITION and this file
-- stopped exiting 0 for an unrelated reason. `opaque_id(n) - 1` satisfies
-- `_ < n` on every input while staying unprovable through the opaque call,
-- and the instantiated `_ < 0` at `shady(0)` still contradicts `takepos`'s
-- `_ >= 0`, so the propagation trap is exactly as armed as before.)
mod M do
  needs IO.Console
  fn opaque_id(n : Int) : Int do n end
  fn shady(n : Int) : {Int | _ < n} do opaque_id(n) - 1 end
  fn takepos(k : {Int | _ >= 0}) : Int do k end
  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) do
    println(int_to_string(takepos(shady(0))))
  end
end
